  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1256 PM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nWarm and dry conditions will continue through the rest of this week.\n\nTuesday will be our warmest day, with mid-80s over the plains and \nlow-70s for the high valleys. Tuesday may also see some scattered \nshower activity over the higher terrain, as a bit more moisture \nmoves in. One or two showers may also push towards I-25, though \ncurrently the chance is somewhat low, and most precipitation will \nstay over the mountains. \n\nThrough the rest of the week, temperatures will cool, with most of \nthe area remaining dry, with only some isolated POPs over the \nmountains again. High temps will largely settle into the 60s to low-\n70s over the valleys, and 70s to low-80s for the plains. \n\nLooking at the end of the week and into this weekend, model \nsolutions begin to diverge, with a much more complicated pattern \nsetting in. A low pressure system will settle over Texas as the main \nportion of the upper jet shifts north. Meanwhile, another low will \nmove towards the west coast amidst some more tropical activity in \nthe Pacific. Long-range guidance still has a pretty wide temperature \nspread for this weekend, with hints at a broad trough moving in from \nthe Pacific Northwest by early next week. While there is still \nplenty of time for this solution to change, latest trends suggest a \nmore amplified wave pattern, and the potential for some more active \nweather.",
